      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. I tend to eat dinner late and sometimes while slouched on the couch\u2014guess that could be a problem! Is being overweight linked to acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 4,
          "text": "Yes, being overweight is indeed linked to acid reflux, or gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). Excess weight, particularly around the abdomen, increases pressure on the stomach, which can cause stomach contents to flow back into the esophagus, leading to reflux symptoms. Even small amounts of weight gain can increase your risk, even if you are still considered to be within a normal weight range. Therefore, maintaining a healthy weight through diet and exercise can significantly help manage and reduce reflux symptoms. Additionally, adopting habits like eating smaller meals, avoiding late-night dinners, and maintaining an upright posture while eating can further alleviate symptoms.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0009-73-15619:3": 0.4403868019580841,
            "clueweb22-en0035-09-14583:5": 0.26777181029319763,
            "clueweb22-en0023-08-01288:3": 0.23044344782829285,
            "clueweb22-en0040-85-04894:17": 0.004152745008468628
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I eat dinner late at night;",
            "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months;"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019ve actually gained a bit of weight since I stopped exercising these past 6 months. Maybe that\u2019s making it worse. Are there any over-the-counter meds for acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, there are several over-the-counter (OTC) medications available for acid reflux. Some common options include:\n\n1. **Antacids**: These neutralize stomach acid and provide quick relief from heartburn. Examples include Tums, Mylanta, and Rolaids.\n\n2. **H2 Blockers**: These reduce the amount of acid your stomach produces and can provide longer-lasting relief. Examples include Pepcid AC (famotidine) and Zantac (ranitidine).\n\n3. **Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)**: These are stronger medications that block acid production and help heal the esophagus. Examples include Prilosec OTC (omeprazole) and Prevacid 24HR (lansoprazole).\n\nIf you find that these medications don\u2019t alleviate your symptoms or if you experience worsening symptoms, it\u2019s important to consult with a healthcare professional. Additionally, addressing your weight gain through lifestyle changes may also help improve your reflux symptoms.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0009-80-09888:5": 1.2693675756454468,
            "clueweb22-en0033-11-03857:10": 0.6382582783699036,
            "clueweb22-en0030-31-06453:13": 0.5406581163406372,
            "clueweb22-en0032-79-13642:7": 0.43634068965911865,
            "clueweb22-en0026-46-15536:5": 0.18959197402000427,
            "clueweb22-en0036-76-02458:5": 0.1827641874551773,
            "clueweb22-en0004-21-11613:3": 0.159361332654953,
            "clueweb22-en0036-76-02458:4": 0.12299409508705139
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I eat dinner late at night",
            "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. How do these medicines actually work to reduce or stop acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Over-the-counter medications for acid reflux work in different ways to alleviate symptoms. Here\u2019s how each type functions:\\n\\n1. **Antacids**: These medications neutralize stomach acid quickly, providing fast relief from heartburn and indigestion. They contain ingredients like calcium, magnesium, or aluminum, which react with stomach acid to form water and other neutral compounds.\\n\\n2. **H2 Blockers**: These medications, such as famotidine (Pepcid) and ranitidine (Zantac), reduce the amount of acid your stomach produces by blocking histamine receptors in the stomach lining. This helps prevent the symptoms of acid reflux from occurring and provides longer-lasting relief than antacids.\\n\\n3. **Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)**: Medications like omeprazole (Prilosec) and lansoprazole (Prevacid) work by inhibiting the proton pumps in the stomach that produce acid. This leads to a significant reduction in stomach acid production, providing relief from more severe symptoms and allowing healing of the esophagus over time.\\n\\nEach of these options can be effective, but if symptoms persist or worsen, consulting a healthcare professional is recommended.",
          "citations": {
