      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Super helpful list! Honestly, eating late is a bad habit of mine\u2014I usually have dinner close to midnight because of work. Could that alone worsen reflux, or is it usually a combo of things?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Eating late at night, especially close to midnight, can significantly worsen acid reflux. This is primarily due to the timing of your meals, which disrupts your body's natural circadian rhythm and affects digestion. When you eat late, your body has to work harder to digest food while you're lying down, which can lead to increased acid production and greater risk of reflux symptoms. Additionally, late-night meals are often heavier and may include trigger foods that exacerbate reflux. It's typically a combination of late eating and other factors, such as meal composition and lifestyle habits, that contribute to reflux issues. To mitigate these effects, try to finish your meals at least two to three hours before bedtime whenever possible.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0016-31-10008:1": -1.36061429977417
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I eat dinner late at night;"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Makes sense, I guess I should change that routine. Out of curiosity, what\u2019s the main reason acid reflux even happens? Is there a specific cause?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "The main reason acid reflux occurs is due to the malfunctioning of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), which is a muscle that normally keeps stomach acid from flowing back into the esophagus. If the LES is weak or relaxes too much, stomach acid can escape, causing the symptoms of acid reflux. Other contributing factors include increased abdominal pressure (from obesity or pregnancy), certain medications, lifestyle habits (like eating late), and specific dietary choices (such as consuming spicy or fatty foods). It's often a combination of these factors that leads to acid reflux symptoms.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0027-76-12162:10": 3.757340431213379,
