      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting, I didn\u2019t realize how much habits could affect it. When it comes to treatment, are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, there are several over-the-counter (OTC) medications available for treating stomach acid reflux. These include:\n\n1. **Antacids**: Medications like Tums\u00ae, Maalox\u00ae, and Mylanta\u00ae neutralize stomach acid for quick, short-term relief.\n\n2. **H2-Receptor Blockers**: These include famotidine (Pepcid AC) and ranitidine (Zantac), which reduce acid production in the stomach and can provide relief for up to 12 hours.\n\n3. **Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)**: OTC options such as omeprazole (Prilosec OTC) and lansoprazole (Prevacid 24 HR) are available and work by blocking acid production, allowing time for the esophagus to heal.\n\nIt's important to consult with a healthcare provider if symptoms persist or worsen, as they can recommend the best treatment for your specific situation.",
