  {
    "runtag": "cfda-chiq-llm4cs-splade-rrf",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "1-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great rundown! How do these meds actually work in the body to stop reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Over-the-counter (OTC) medications for acid reflux work in different ways to alleviate symptoms. Here\u2019s how each type functions in the body:\n\n1. **Antacids**: These medications, like Tums and Maalox, neutralize existing stomach acid, providing quick relief from heartburn and discomfort. They act almost immediately by raising the pH level in the stomach, which helps to reduce irritation in the esophagus.\n\n2. **H2-Receptor Blockers**: Medications such as famotidine (Pepcid) and ranitidine (Zantac) reduce the amount of acid produced by the stomach. They work by blocking histamine receptors in the stomach lining, which decreases acid secretion and helps prevent symptoms for several hours.\n\n3. **Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)**: OTC options like omeprazole (Prilosec) and lansoprazole (Prevacid) inhibit the proton pumps in the stomach lining that produce acid. This results in a significant reduction in stomach acid over time, allowing for healing of the esophagus and longer-lasting relief from reflux symptoms.\n\nEach of these medications targets acid reflux differently, and the choice of which to use can depend",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:3": 4.050055503845215,
