          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I eat dinner late at night.",
            "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.",
            "I'm a social person.",
            "I want to change my lifestyle."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s super relevant for me, I actually haven\u2019t exercised in six months and I\u2019ve noticed some weight gain. I guess that could be worsening my symptoms. Is stress related to acid reflux as well?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Yes, stress can indeed play a role in worsening acid reflux symptoms. While stress doesn\u2019t directly cause acid reflux, it can increase your sensitivity to stomach acid, making the symptoms feel more intense. Stress may also disrupt the function of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), the muscle that prevents stomach acid from flowing back into the esophagus. Additionally, stress can lead to behaviors like overeating or eating trigger foods, which can further aggravate reflux.\n\nTo manage both stress and acid reflux, lifestyle changes can be very effective. Incorporating relaxation techniques such as yoga or meditation, which you might find appealing given your interest in lifestyle changes, can help reduce stress. Regular exercise, even low-impact activities like walking, can also alleviate stress and support weight management, which is beneficial for reflux. Since you\u2019re looking to change your lifestyle, these adjustments could be a great starting point for improving both your physical and mental well-being.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-50-07413:3": 39.47060012817383,
            "clueweb22-en0038-68-03750:5": 38.10960006713867,
            "clueweb22-en0008-04-03010:2": 37.970401763916016
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.",
            "I want to change my lifestyle."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s pretty helpful, thanks! So I guess being stressed, gaining weight, and eating late all make it worse for me. What exactly causes acid reflux in the body though? Like, why does it even happen?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Acid reflux occurs when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), a muscle that acts as a valve between your stomach and esophagus, malfunctions. Normally, the LES closes tightly after food passes into the stomach, preventing stomach acid from flowing back up. However, if the LES is weakened or relaxes inappropriately, stomach acid can escape into the esophagus, causing the burning sensation known as heartburn. This malfunction can be triggered by factors like weight gain, which increases abdominal pressure, or eating late at night, which allows acid to flow more easily when lying down. Stress can also exacerbate symptoms by increasing sensitivity to stomach acid. Over time, repeated acid exposure can damage the esophagus and lead to chronic conditions like GERD. Addressing triggers such as meal timing, weight management, and stress reduction can help mitigate symptoms.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-81-17497:1": 34.76380157470703,
            "clueweb22-en0032-81-17497:2": 30.643999099731445
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I eat dinner late at night.",
            "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Oh, I see. So the LES is like a leaky valve sometimes. Makes sense. Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, there are several over-the-counter (OTC) medications available to help manage acid reflux. Antacids like Tums, Maalox, and Mylanta can provide quick relief by neutralizing stomach acid. H2 receptor blockers, such as famotidine (Pepcid), reduce acid production and are effective for longer-lasting relief. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) like omeprazole (Prilosec) and lansoprazole (Prevacid) are also available OTC and work by blocking acid production more effectively, though they may take a few days to show full results. If your symptoms persist despite using these medications, it\u2019s a good idea to consult a doctor for further evaluation and treatment options.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0042-66-19101:3": 39.472999572753906,
            "clueweb22-en0034-45-15635:3": 36.25740051269531,
            "clueweb22-en0042-29-01505:0": 35.392398834228516
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I eat dinner late at night.",
            "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.",
            "I want to change my lifestyle."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s good to know. How do those meds actually work on stomach acid?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Acid reflux medications work in different ways to manage stomach acid. Antacids, like Tums or Mylanta, provide quick relief by neutralizing existing stomach acid, but their effects are temporary and do not prevent further acid production. H2 blockers, such as famotidine (Pepcid) or ranitidine, reduce acid production by blocking histamine receptors in the stomach lining, offering longer relief\u2014up to 12 hours\u2014and can be taken before meals to prevent symptoms. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs), like omeprazole or lansoprazole, are stronger and work by shutting down the stomach's acid-producing pumps. They take longer to become effective, often requiring daily use for several days, but provide long-lasting relief and help heal damage caused by chronic reflux. Each type of medication targets acid differently, so the choice depends on your symptoms and needs.",
