          "user_utterance": "For quick relief of acid reflux symptoms, antacids such as Tums, Rolaids, Mylanta, and Maalox are widely available and work by neutralizing stomach acid. H2 receptor blockers, including Pepcid AC (famotidine) and Tagamet (cimetidine), reduce acid production and provide longer-lasting relief. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) like Prilosec (omeprazole) and Prevacid (lansoprazole) are also available OTC and are effective for frequent or persistent symptoms by significantly reducing stomach acid production. Side effects of these medications are usually mild but can include headache, diarrhea, or upset stomach. If OTC medications do not provide relief, or if you need them regularly, it's important to consult a healthcare provider for further evaluation and possible prescription options [clueweb22-en0009-46-05873][clueweb22-en0020-48-12159][clueweb22-en0009-09-09722]. Are there any interactions between these medications and other drugs?",
