      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That didn\u2019t really answer my question. I already know the types of medications, but I\u2019d like to know *how* they work in the body to help with acid reflux. Can you explain that more specifically?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Antacids, like Tums or Mylanta, work by directly neutralizing stomach acid with alkaline minerals such as calcium or magnesium, providing quick but short-term relief. H2 blockers (e.g., famotidine, cimetidine) work by blocking histamine H2 receptors on stomach cells, which reduces the signal for acid secretion and provides longer-lasting relief\u2014about 12 hours\u2014by decreasing acid production at its source. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs), such as omeprazole, act by blocking the proton pumps in the stomach lining, which are responsible for the final step in acid production, making them the most effective at reducing overall acid levels for longer periods. Each class works at a different stage of acid production or neutralization, which is why they vary in speed and duration of relief [clueweb22-en0043-05-13136][clueweb22-en0010-70-01256][clueweb22-en0002-30-06103][clueweb22-en0029-78-09970].",
