      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "What are some common myths about acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Common Myths About Acid Reflux\u2014Debunked for Your Needs\n\n**Myth 1: Acid reflux is only caused by spicy or acidic foods.**  \nReality: While spicy, fatty, and acidic foods (like tomatoes, citrus, onions) are common triggers, reflux is highly individual. Even healthy foods (e.g., some fruits, nuts, or dairy) can trigger symptoms for certain people (clueweb22-en0041-22-06879, clueweb22-en0038-68-03750). Track your own reactions, especially with favorites like mango and pomegranate.\n\n**Myth 2: Eating late at night always causes weight gain and reflux.**  \nReality: Late meals increase reflux risk because lying down soon after eating allows acid to flow back up (clueweb22-en0032-81-17497). However, weight gain is about total daily calories, not meal timing (clueweb22-en0038-36-06652). For reflux, finish eating 2\u20133 hours before bed.\n\n**Myth 3:",
