          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For persistent or severe acid reflux\u2014especially with your late dinners and weight management goals\u2014prescription medications are often the most effective option when lifestyle changes and OTC remedies aren\u2019t enough.\n\n**Best Prescription Medications for Acid Reflux**\n\n- **Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs):**  \n  - **Most effective for frequent, moderate-to-severe symptoms.**  \n  - **Examples:** Omeprazole (Prilosec), Esomeprazole (Nexium), Lansoprazole (Prevacid), Pantoprazole (Protonix), Rabeprazole (Aciphex), Dexlansoprazole (Dexilant).  \n  - **How they work:** Strongly reduce stomach acid production, allowing the esophagus to heal.  \n  - **Fit for you:** PPIs are ideal if you have ongoing symptoms despite dietary changes. They\u2019re safe for most people but should be used at the lowest effective dose, especially long-term (possible risks: vitamin/mineral deficiencies, bone health).\n\n- **H2 Blockers (Prescription",
