            "clueweb22-en0007-51-09036:7": 4.400695323944092
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. I have been eating late at night a lot, so maybe I\u2019ll try moving dinner earlier. Does gaining weight make acid reflux worse?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some good habits to help someone with stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Yes, gaining weight can make acid reflux worse. Excess weight, particularly around the stomach area, can put additional pressure on the stomach, causing stomach acid to flow back up into the esophagus, leading to heartburn and other acid reflux symptoms. Maintaining a healthy weight through a balanced diet and regular exercise can help alleviate acid reflux symptoms. It's also important to note that eating late at night and then lying down can worsen symptoms, so adjusting your eating schedule, as you mentioned, can also be beneficial.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0038-68-03750:6": 5.088813781738281,
            "clueweb22-en0010-23-06105:5": 4.642402648925781,
            "clueweb22-en0027-29-03071:3": 4.452937126159668,
            "clueweb22-en0018-40-11899:10": 4.124355316162109,
            "clueweb22-en0003-33-09589:2": 4.121144771575928
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Hmm, I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising. Do you have any suggestions for someone in my position to improve acid reflux and maybe get back into better habits?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some good habits to help someone with stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To improve acid reflux and get back into better habits, consider starting with low-impact exercises like cycling, gentle runs, or walking. These activities can help you lose weight and reduce abdominal pressure without putting too much strain on your stomach. Additionally, try to establish a consistent exercise routine and combine it with a balanced diet and healthy eating habits, such as avoiding trigger foods, eating smaller meals, and not eating too close to bedtime. This holistic approach can help alleviate acid reflux symptoms and support your overall well-being. It's also essential to monitor your body's response to different exercises and foods to make adjustments as needed. Consult with a healthcare professional for personalized advice on managing acid reflux and getting back into shape.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-81-17497:10": 2.7213683128356934,
            "clueweb22-en0025-49-01770:8": 2.0941574573516846,
            "clueweb22-en0016-95-18158:6": 1.6972713470458984,
            "clueweb22-en0026-53-16574:3": 1.5712475776672363,
            "clueweb22-en0003-81-14402:1": 1.5575271844863892
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. Is spicy food a big trigger for reflux? I eat out a lot, so just wondering if that\u2019s something I should watch for.",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Spicy food can be a trigger for acid reflux for some people, but it's not a universal trigger. While some studies suggest that spicy foods can trigger abdominal pain, the evidence is mixed, and individual tolerance to spices varies greatly. If you're concerned, it's a good idea to be mindful of your spice intake, especially when eating out, and see how your body reacts. You may find that you can tolerate certain levels of spiciness without issue, or you may need to avoid it altogether to manage your acid reflux symptoms. It's also worth noting that other factors like fatty, fried, or high-fat foods, as well as chocolate, coffee, and citrus fruits, are more commonly associated with triggering acid reflux.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0018-30-10981:2": 6.528763771057129,
            "clueweb22-en0044-72-05971:4": 6.491393089294434,
            "clueweb22-en0003-88-05119:4": 5.9591450691223145,
            "clueweb22-en0018-97-08308:6": 5.752165794372559,
            "clueweb22-en0009-95-06055:9": 5.705961227416992
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! That\u2019s good to know since I like trying new restaurants. Why does acid reflux happen though? Is there one main reason it starts?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Acid reflux occurs when the lower esophageal sphincter, a muscle between the stomach and esophagus, malfunctions, allowing stomach acid to flow back into the esophagus. This can be triggered by various factors, including diet, weight, sleeping posture, stomach abnormalities, pregnancy, stress, and genetics. While there isn't one main reason it starts, these factors can contribute to the condition. For example, eating large meals, lying down after eating, or wearing tight clothing can trigger acid reflux. Additionally, certain foods like citrus fruits, spicy or fatty foods, and chocolate can also trigger symptoms. It's often a combination of these factors that leads to acid reflux, rather than a single cause.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-50-07413:1": 6.333861827850342,
            "clueweb22-en0009-92-03567:3": 5.852705001831055,
            "clueweb22-en0032-81-17497:2": 5.846325397491455,
            "clueweb22-en0034-50-07413:2": 5.643156051635742,
            "clueweb22-en0039-65-00093:1": 5.253500938415527
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 10,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting, I didn\u2019t know there were so many factors. Are there any medications you can get without a prescription for reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, there are several over-the-counter medications available to help treat acid reflux. These include antacids, histamine-2 (H2) blockers, and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs). Antacids can provide quick relief from heartburn, while H2 blockers and PPIs can reduce acid production in the stomach. Some examples of these medications include Tums, Maalox, ranitidine, famotidine, and omeprazole. It's essential to follow the instructions and consult with a healthcare professional if your symptoms persist or worsen. Additionally, medications like cimetidine, nizatidine, and lansoprazole are also available over-the-counter to help with acid reflux.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0002-04-09789:3": 9.882108688354492,
            "clueweb22-en0017-96-03792:10": 8.36417007446289,
            "clueweb22-en0022-58-03910:4": 8.05985164642334,
            "clueweb22-en0017-09-02813:0": 8.04554557800293,
            "clueweb22-en0027-47-12028:2": 7.907695770263672
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 11,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How do those medications actually work to help with reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Those medications work by either neutralizing stomach acid, reducing acid production, or blocking the production of stomach acid. Antacids provide quick relief by neutralizing stomach acid, while H2 blockers reduce acid production in the stomach. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) block the production of stomach acid, providing longer-lasting relief. By reducing the amount of acid in the stomach, these medications help alleviate heartburn and other acid reflux symptoms. It's essential to follow the instructions and consult with a healthcare professional to determine the best course of treatment for your specific condition.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0022-22-09431:6": 8.26205062866211,
            "clueweb22-en0022-71-15890:17": 7.60786247253418,
