          "text": "Yes, weight gain can make acid reflux worse. When you gain weight, it can put extra pressure on your stomach, causing",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0027-19-00865:3": 6.107426643371582,
            "clueweb22-en0004-21-11613:2": 6.028586387634277,
            "clueweb22-en0004-21-11613:1": 5.5383195877075195,
            "clueweb22-en0027-19-00865:2": 5.212146759033203,
            "clueweb22-en0042-77-18751:15": 5.181300163269043
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Got it, that explains a lot. Why does acid reflux happen in the first place? What's the cause behind it?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Acid reflux occurs when stomach acid flows back up into the esophagus, causing heartburn and discomfort. The cause behind it can be multifaceted, but it's often related to a combination of factors such as low stomach acid, bacterial overgrowth in the gut, and increased abdominal pressure. This pressure can be exacerbated by factors like weight gain, eating large or late meals, and certain food choices. Additionally, factors like stress, tight clothing, and neurological issues can also contribute to acid reflux. It's not necessarily caused by excess stomach acid, but rather by the stomach acid being in the wrong place at the wrong time.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0005-28-07259:2": 5.771556377410889,
            "clueweb22-en0025-26-18393:3": 5.3136210441589355,
            "clueweb22-en0004-44-03413:5": 5.313543319702148,
            "clueweb22-en0025-87-14378:2": 4.878321647644043,
            "clueweb22-en0040-71-07591:7": 4.811168193817139
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Makes sense. Are there any OTC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, there are several over-the-counter (OTC) medications available for acid reflux. These include antacids, H2-receptor blockers, and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs). Antacids help neutralize stomach acid, while H2-receptor blockers reduce acid production. Proton pump inhibitors block acid production and are often used for more severe cases. Examples of OTC medications for acid reflux include Tums, Rolaids, and Mylanta, as well as famotidine (Pepcid AC) and omeprazole (Prilosec OTC). It's always a good idea to talk to your doctor before starting any new medication, especially if you have a history of stomach problems or are taking other medications.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-09-02813:0": 8.798521995544434,
            "clueweb22-en0044-99-14195:8": 8.130475997924805,
            "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:0": 7.50366735458374,
            "clueweb22-en0009-98-01666:0": 7.168360710144043,
            "clueweb22-en0016-97-03528:3": 7.125612258911133
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Nice, thanks! So, how exactly do those medications work in the body to help with acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Those medications work by either neutralizing stomach acid or reducing its production. Antacids provide immediate relief by neutralizing stomach acid. H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors, on the other hand, reduce the amount of acid produced by the stomach. H2 blockers block the substance that stimulates the stomach to produce gastric acid, while proton pump inhibitors block the proton pump in the stomach wall responsible for stomach acid production. By reducing acid production, these medications help prevent stomach acid from flowing back up into the esophagus, which can cause heartburn and discomfort. This helps alleviate symptoms of acid reflux and provides relief.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0022-22-09431:6": 8.068737983703613,
            "clueweb22-en0022-71-15890:17": 7.829244136810303,
            "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 7.422444820404053,
            "clueweb22-en0013-07-13300:7": 7.401107311248779,
            "clueweb22-en0022-92-12426:13": 7.222611427307129
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
