      {
        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. How do these medications actually work in the body to relieve acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "These medications work by either neutralizing stomach acid, reducing acid production, or blocking acid production altogether. Antacids, for example, contain ingredients like calcium carbonate that help neutralize stomach acid. H2 receptor antagonists, on the other hand, reduce acid production in the stomach by blocking histamine receptors. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) block the production of acid in the stomach by inhibiting the enzyme system that produces stomach acid. By reducing or blocking acid production, these medications can help alleviate symptoms of acid reflux, such as heartburn and discomfort. However, it's essential to note that long-term use of these medications can lead to nutrient deficiencies, such as zinc, calcium, and magnesium deficiencies. It's crucial to follow the recommended dosage and consult with your doctor if you experience any side effects or concerns.   Please let me rephrase the answer to fit the 200-word limit: These medications work by either neutralizing stomach acid, reducing acid production, or blocking acid production",
