        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thanks! Why does acid reflux actually happen? Is there usually an underlying reason for it?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Acid reflux happens when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) relaxes at the wrong time, allowing stomach acid to flow back up into the esophagus. This can be due to various reasons, including a weak LES, increased acid production in the stomach, or indigestion. However, in some cases, underlying causes such as low stomach acid, small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO), or excess histamine production can also contribute to acid reflux. It's essential to identify the underlying cause to effectively manage and treat acid reflux. Consider consulting with a healthcare professional for personalized advice. Get back to exercising regularly, and you'll likely see an improvement in your symptoms. Remember to also eat smaller, more frequent meals, and avoid lying down after eating to help prevent acid reflux.  Consider keeping a food diary to track which foods affect you the most. Consult with your doctor for personalized recommendations.  Also, be mindful of your snacking and eating habits, especially before",
