      {
        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s a good list. Can you explain how those medications for acid reflux actually work?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Antacids work by directly neutralizing stomach acid. H2-receptor blockers reduce acid production by blocking histamine's signal to acid-producing cells in the stomach. Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) are stronger, irreversibly blocking the \"proton pump\" (H+/K+-ATPase) in stomach cells, which is the final step in acid production. Alginates neutralize acid and form a protective gel barrier on top of stomach contents to prevent reflux.",
          "citations": {
