      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting, thanks! Why does acid reflux actually happen\u2014what goes wrong in the body for it to start?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Acid reflux primarily occurs when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), a muscular valve between your esophagus and stomach, weakens or relaxes inappropriately. Normally, the LES closes tightly after food enters your stomach to prevent contents from flowing back. However, when it malfunctions, stomach acid and contents can flow back up into the esophagus, causing irritation and symptoms like heartburn.",
