      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. How do these medications actually work in the body to reduce acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Over-the-counter acid reflux medications work in different ways:\n*   **Antacids** neutralize existing stomach acid for quick relief.\n*   **Alginates** form a gel-like raft that floats on stomach acid, creating a protective barrier to prevent it from entering the esophagus.\n*   **H2-blockers** (like famotidine) reduce stomach acid production by blocking histamine signals that tell stomach cells to secrete acid.\n*   **Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)** (like omeprazole) are stronger and reduce acid by blocking the action of specific enzymes or \"pumps\" in stomach cells responsible for acid production.",
          "citations": {
