      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That actually makes sense, especially since I\u2019ve gained a bit recently after not exercising for months. Why does acid reflux actually happen? What\u2019s the reason?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Acid reflux primarily occurs when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), a muscle that typically keeps stomach acid in your stomach, weakens or relaxes improperly. This allows stomach acid to flow back up into your esophagus. Factors contributing to this include increased abdominal pressure from weight gain or pregnancy, certain foods (fatty, spicy, acidic), overeating, eating too close to bedtime, smoking, and conditions like a hiatal hernia. Some theories also suggest that a lack of stomach acid can contribute.",
          "citations": {
