      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Cool, thanks. How do these medications actually work in the body to relieve reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Antacids (e.g., Tums) work by neutralizing existing stomach acid for quick relief. H2 blockers (e.g., famotidine) reduce acid production by blocking histamine receptors in the stomach lining. Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) like omeprazole are more potent, working by shutting down the \"proton pumps\" in stomach cells that produce acid, significantly lowering acid levels.",
