    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I want to know what foods are good or bad for acid reflux. Can you share a list or some tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "What  food is good for stomach acid reflux and some that I should avoid?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here \u2019 s a quick, practical guide. often better - tolerated - oatmeal and other whole grains - non - citrus fruits : bananas, melons ( incl. watermelon ), apples ; try mango in moderation earlier in the day - vegetables : leafy greens, cucumbers, broccoli, green beans - lean proteins : fish, chicken / turkey breast, tofu, egg whites ; beans if they don \u2019 t bloat you - low - fat dairy or yogurt ( if tolerated ) - ginger, almonds common triggers - spicy dishes ; fried / greasy foods - high - fat meats / cheese ; chocolate ; mint - tomato products ( pizza / marinara ), citrus, pineapple - coffee / energy drinks, alcohol, carbonated drinks - large meals or eating late at night pizzeria / eating - out tips - choose thin crust, light cheese, more veggies ; consider white pizza / olive oil instead of tomato sauce - pick grilled / baked over fried ; ask for sauces on the side - avoid late - night pizza \u2014 finish dinner 3 + hours before bed habits that help - smaller, more frequent meals ; don \u2019 t overeat - stay upright after",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0030-92-07136:11": -0.8442258834838867,
            "clueweb22-en0003-47-01476:5": -0.868097186088562,
            "clueweb22-en0004-94-03520:10": -0.8834503293037415,
            "clueweb22-en0008-48-18557:15": -0.9247082471847534,
            "clueweb22-en0025-12-07573:5": -0.9494452476501465
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s super useful, thanks! I see apples, watermelon, and bananas are good options\u2014love those. Is kiwi okay too if you have reflux, or should it be avoided?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some fruits good for acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer: kiwi is more acidic than apples, bananas, or melon, so it can trigger reflux for some people. It\u2019s not off-limits, but test your tolerance.  Tips if you want to include kiwi: - Try a small portion (\u00bd\u20131 fruit) earlier in the day, not within 3\u20134 hours of bedtime. - Eat it with a buffer like oatmeal, low\u2011fat yogurt/Greek yogurt, or cottage cheese to reduce irritation and add protein. - Choose very ripe/sweeter (gold) kiwis, which tend to be gentler than tart, unripe green ones. - Avoid kiwi juice and large servings. - If you notice heartburn within a couple of hours after eating it, skip it\u2014stick to lower\u2011acid fruits (bananas, melon, most apples, berries, stone fruit).  During flare\u2011ups, it\u2019s safer to avoid kiwi altogether.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0018-15-01332:5": -3.086015224456787,
            "clueweb22-en0041-54-06342:6": -3.1954925060272217,
            "clueweb22-en0041-22-06879:4": -3.7978930473327637,
            "clueweb22-en0018-15-01332:4": -3.872809648513794,
            "clueweb22-en0013-12-08084:8": -3.9054791927337646
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Got it, thanks. I\u2019ll try having kiwi with oatmeal, maybe for breakfast. What about habits besides food\u2014like daily routines or post-meal stuff\u2014to help reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some good habits to help someone with stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great plan. beyond food, these habits help a lot : - meal timing / size : eat smaller, more frequent meals. finish dinner 3 \u2013 4 hours before bed ; avoid late - night snacks. don \u2019 t overeat. - after meals : stay upright at least 2 \u2013 3 hours. take a gentle walk. avoid bending, crunches, or heavy lifting for a few hours. - sleep : sleep on your left side with your head elevated ( wedge pillow or elevate bed 6 \u2013 8 inches ). a recliner at ~ 45\u00b0 can help on bad days. - weight and activity : aim for daily movement ( e. g., 30 minutes brisk walking ). gradual weight loss reduces pressure on your stomach. - stress : do 10 minutes of deep breathing, meditation, or yoga daily \u2014 stress can worsen reflux. - drinks : limit alcohol, caffeine, and carbonated beverages \u2014 especially late. - clothing / posture : avoid tight belts / waistbands ; sit and stand tall. - hydration : sip water between meals ; avoid large fluids right before bedtime. - tech habits : set a phone - free cutoff after dinner to prevent late - night snacking and lying down right after eating.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0027-58-19039:6": 2.1005818843841553,
            "clueweb22-en0002-19-16904:12": 1.8862154483795166,
            "clueweb22-en0041-36-06415:6": 1.7866487503051758,
            "clueweb22-en0038-68-03750:10": 1.5542906522750854,
            "clueweb22-en0034-50-07413:4": 1.5346404314041138
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting\u2014you mentioned tech habits and a phone-free cutoff after dinner. I spend a lot of time on my phone, especially doom scrolling late at night. Could this make my reflux worse?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "short answer : yes \u2014 late - night doomscrolling can worsen reflux, mostly indirectly. why it hurts : - delays bedtime, so you \u2019 re lying down soon after dinner \u2014 prime time for reflux. - encourages late - night snacking and bigger portions. - stress / anxiety from news feeds heightens symptoms. - slouched / curled posture compresses your abdomen. - blue light delays sleep, and poor sleep can worsen reflux and weight gain. make it reflux - friendly : - set a phone cutoff 3 \u2013 4 hours before bed ; charge it outside the bedroom. - use android digital wellbeing : bedtime mode, focus mode after dinner, app timers, grayscale, and silence notifications. - replace the habit : 10 \u2013 20 minute post \u2011 dinner walk, warm shower, paper book, gentle stretches / breathing, or call a friend. - if you must use your phone : sit upright, avoid lying on your right side, enable night mode, set a 10 \u2013 15 minute timer, and don \u2019 t snack ; sip water instead. - aim to finish dinner earlier and smaller ; stay upright 2 \u2013 3 hours after meals. these tweaks help both ref",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0036-65-05290:9": -4.30125617980957,
            "clueweb22-en0034-09-00524:3": -4.338323593139648,
            "clueweb22-en0032-75-16058:13": -4.446371078491211,
            "clueweb22-en0045-55-00316:3": -4.5493927001953125,
            "clueweb22-en0004-21-11613:0": -4.563742637634277
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Very helpful tips\u2014digital wellbeing tools sound like an easy start! I usually eat dinner late and then go online, so I\u2019ll try cutting screen time and moving my dinner earlier. \n\nWhy does acid reflux happen in the first place? Does it have a root cause?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short answer : reflux happens when stomach contents flow back into the esophagus because the lower esophageal sphincter ( les ) relaxes or is weak, and gravity can \u2019 t keep acid down \u2014 especially when you lie down after eating. what drives it - timing / position : large or late meals, then lying down ( less help from gravity ). - les dysfunction : transient relaxations or a chronically weak valve ; hiatal hernia is common. - pressure in the abdomen : weight gain, tight waistbands, pregnancy, constipation, big meals. - slower stomach emptying. - diet triggers for some : fatty / fried / spicy foods, chocolate, mint, coffee / energy drinks, alcohol, carbonated drinks, tomato / citrus. - smoking and certain meds ( e. g., nsaids / aspirin, some calcium \u2011 channel blockers, nitrates, anticholinergics ) can worsen symptoms. - stress and poor sleep amplify perception of symptoms. is there one root cause? usually it \u2019 s multifactorial. chronic gerd often reflects les / hiatal hernia plus lifestyle triggers. acid \u2011 suppressing meds reduce acidity",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-52-07852:3": -6.133845329284668,
            "clueweb22-en0004-21-11613:0": -6.167740821838379,
            "clueweb22-en0017-49-06457:7": -6.280145645141602,
            "clueweb22-en0015-72-12318:3": -6.437772274017334,
            "clueweb22-en0044-33-12571:0": -6.551546096801758
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Makes sense. You mentioned acid-suppressing meds\u2014are there any over-the-counter (OTC) options specifically for treating stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "yes \u2014 three otc categories can help : - antacids : tums, maalox, mylanta, gaviscon. fast relief in minutes but short - acting ( 1 \u2013 3 hours ). good for occasional heartburn ; don \u2019 t heal irritation. - h2 blockers : famotidine ( pepcid ac ), cimetidine ( tagamet ), nizatidine ( axid ). start working in about an hour, last up to 12 hours. useful before trigger meals or at bedtime. ( ranitidine is no longer available. ) - proton pump inhibitors ( ppis ) : omeprazole ( prilosec otc ), lansoprazole ( prevacid 24hr ), esomeprazole ( nexium 24hr ). strongest option ; take once daily 30 \u2013 60 minutes before breakfast for a 14 \u2011 day course. may take 1 \u2013 4 days for full effect ; best for symptoms occurring > 2 times / week. safety notes : - avoid frequent, long - term use without medical guidance ; chronic acid suppression can lower vitamin b12 absorption. - cimetidine has more drug interactions \u2014 check labels if you take other",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0038-38-12773:13": 1.1449954509735107,
            "clueweb22-en0016-83-09082:12": 0.37843236327171326,
            "clueweb22-en0015-98-04013:9": 0.3603544533252716,
            "clueweb22-en0027-47-12028:2": 0.2134515643119812,
            "clueweb22-en0023-53-19956:15": 0.19937704503536224
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for the breakdown! How exactly do these medications work to control acid reflux?",
