          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Eating dinner late at night might be making it worse for me then. Why does acid reflux actually happen, by the way?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Good point \u2014 late dinners often do make reflux worse. In short, acid reflux happens when stomach contents move backward into the esophagus because the body\u2019s normal barriers or drainage aren\u2019t working well. Key reasons:  - Weak or relaxed lower esophageal sphincter (LES): the valve between esophagus and stomach should close after a meal. If it relaxes too often or is damaged, acid can escape.   - Increased stomach pressure or volume: large, fatty, or late meals expand the stomach and push contents up.   - Delayed gastric emptying: if food stays in the stomach longer, there\u2019s more opportunity to reflux.   - Body position: when you lie down soon after eating, gravity no longer keeps contents down, so reflux reaches the esophagus and throat more easily.   - Vicious cycle: repeated reflux inflames and stiffens the sphincter and esophagus, making reflux more likely over time.  That\u2019s why smaller meals, finishing eating 2\u20133 hours before bed, elevating the head of the bed, and weight reduction help. If symptoms persist, see your doctor.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-65-01816:9": 36.44110107421875,
            "clueweb22-en0034-44-00447:0": 36.13850021362305,
            "clueweb22-en0004-09-00342:1": 35.347801208496094,
            "clueweb22-en0045-20-06751:13": 35.08440017700195,
            "clueweb22-en0009-57-13764:2": 34.92570114135742
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Got it, thanks! Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 several OTC options are available:  - Antacids (quick relief): Tums, Rolaids, Mylanta, Maalox, Gaviscon, Pepto\u2011Bismol. Chew or dissolve tablets (some come as liquids).   - H2 blockers (reduce acid production): famotidine (Pepcid AC), cimetidine (Tagamet HB), nizatidine (Zantac 360). Good for short\u2011term or occasional heartburn.   - Proton\u2011pump inhibitors (PPIs \u2014 stronger acid suppression): omeprazole (Prilosec OTC), lansoprazole (Prevacid 24HR), esomeprazole (Nexium 24HR). OTC PPIs are usually taken for a 14\u2011day course for frequent heartburn.  Combination OTCs: Pepcid Complete (antacid + H2) and Zegerid OTC (PPI + bicarbonate).  Use as directed on the label. Antacids for immediate relief; H2s/PPIs for ongoing control. Don\u2019t self\u2011treat longer than recommended \u2014 see your doctor if symptoms worsen or persist past the labeled duration (usually >2 weeks). Be aware of side effects, drug interactions, pregnancy/breastfeeding issues, and that some older drugs (e.g., ranitidine) were removed from the market.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 68.00180053710938,
