      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How do those medications actually reduce acid reflux symptoms?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "They work in three different ways:  - Antacids (Tums, Rolaids, Mylanta) chemically neutralize stomach acid on contact. That raises pH quickly and gives fast, short\u2011lived relief of burning, but doesn\u2019t stop new acid production or heal damaged tissue.  - H2\u2011receptor blockers (famotidine/Pepcid, cimetidine) block histamine H2 receptors on stomach parietal cells, which reduces the amount of acid the stomach produces. They take longer than antacids (often ~1 hour) but last many hours and are useful for occasional or predictable heartburn.  - Proton\u2011pump inhibitors (omeprazole, esomeprazole, lansoprazole) block the stomach\u2019s final acid\u2011secreting step (the H+/K+ ATPase \u201cproton pump\u201d), profoundly reducing acid production. They\u2019re the most powerful for healing inflammation and preventing recurrent reflux but take longer (often 1\u20134 days to reach full effect) and are usually taken daily for a prescribed course.  Choice depends on symptom timing, severity, and whether tissue healing is needed.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0045-30-11232:3": 67.08760070800781,
            "clueweb22-en0015-98-04013:9": 62.382301330566406,
