    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Cool, that\u2019s helpful\u2014watermelon and apples are two of my favorites! Any habits besides food choices that might help with reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some good habits to help someone with stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 several non\u2011food habits can make a big difference:  - Eat smaller meals and eat more often; avoid overeating.   - Eat slowly to prevent getting too full.   - Stop eating 2\u20133 hours before lying down or going to bed; don\u2019t lie down for at least 2 hours after a meal.   - Elevate the head of your bed about 10\u201320 cm (4\u20138 inches) to reduce nighttime reflux.   - Quit smoking and limit or avoid alcohol and caffeine (they relax the LES and increase reflux).   - Avoid tight clothes that press on your abdomen.   - Maintain a healthy weight and exercise regularly (but avoid intense exercise right after meals).   - Keep a simple food/symptom diary to identify personal triggers and portion effects.  If lifestyle changes don\u2019t control symptoms, talk with your doctor \u2014 medical treatments (like proton pump inhibitors) can be very effective for moderate to severe reflux.",
