  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "1-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m curious about foods for acid reflux\u2014what should I eat or avoid?",
          "user_rubrik": "What  food is good for stomach acid reflux and some that I should avoid?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Good question \u2014 small diet and habit changes can make a big difference.  Foods to avoid (common triggers) - Fried, greasy, high\u2011fat foods and large servings of red meat (stay away from fatty cuts) - Chocolate, peppermint/spearmint, coffee/caffeinated drinks, alcohol - Citrus, tomato and tomato sauces, carbonated drinks, spicy/very oniony or garlicky meals  Foods to eat more of - Lean proteins: fish, skinless poultry, legumes/beans, tofu - High\u2011fiber whole grains and veggies: oatmeal, brown rice, sweet potato, broccoli, leafy greens - Non\u2011citrus fruits: apples, watermelon, kiwi; moderate avocado/healthy fats (olive oil, walnuts) - Fermented foods with probiotics: yogurt, kefir, miso, sauerkraut (may help digestion) - Nuts (almonds) and B\u2011vitamin/calcium sources like spinach and fortified cereals  Lifestyle tips - Avoid overeating; have smaller, frequent meals - Don\u2019t eat within 2\u20133 hours of lying down (especially since you eat late) - Eat slowly, stay hydrated, lose excess weight, avoid smoking and limit alcohol  If symptoms persist or worsen, see your provider for testing or meds.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-66-13847:3": 3.1197783946990967,
            "clueweb22-en0003-41-13624:10": 3.031525135040283,
            "clueweb22-en0012-86-13333:4": 2.785066604614258,
            "clueweb22-en0017-52-15217:2": 2.7350404262542725,
            "clueweb22-en0021-74-19197:5": 2.4529378414154053
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thank you. Watermelon and kiwi are some of my favorites, so I\u2019m happy to see those listed! Are bananas also good for acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some fruits good for acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 for most people bananas are a reflux-friendly choice. They're low-acid, high in fiber and potassium, and act like a gentle natural antacid. Ripe bananas are easiest on the stomach.  A few practical tips: - Pair a banana with a protein (low\u2011fat yogurt, kefir, or a spoon of almond/peanut butter) to boost protein and keep you full.   - If dairy bothers you, try plant-based yogurt or just the banana.   - Pay attention to your own reaction \u2014 a small number of people find bananas trigger their GERD, so avoid if you notice symptoms after eating them.   - Keep portion sizes moderate and don\u2019t lie down right after eating (try to stop eating 2\u20133 hours before bed).  If reflux persists despite these changes, check with your provider.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0041-54-06342:6": -2.320711374282837,
            "clueweb22-en0015-18-13111:5": -2.861471176147461,
            "clueweb22-en0020-88-18551:6": -2.8734078407287598,
            "clueweb22-en0003-96-06071:16": -3.0012173652648926,
            "clueweb22-en0004-87-10281:9": -3.2548186779022217
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Got it. Are there any fruits I should definitely avoid if I get reflux? I love a variety of fruits and don\u2019t want too many restrictions, but want to be careful.",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some fruits good for acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great question \u2014 you don\u2019t need to cut out most fruit. The ones to be cautious about are the higher\u2011acid fruits that commonly trigger reflux in many people:  - Citrus: oranges, grapefruit, tangerines, lemons, limes (and their juices)   - Tomato (and tomato\u2011based sauces) \u2014 botanically a fruit and a common trigger   - Pineapple and other very tart fruits can bother some people  Safe, reflux\u2011friendly options for most people: - Bananas, melons (watermelon, cantaloupe), apples, pears, berries, kiwi \u2014 especially when ripe and eaten in moderate portions.  Practical tips: - Prefer whole fruit to concentrated juices; avoid citrus juices and sports drinks with citric acid.   - Eat fruit earlier in the evening and not right before bed.   - Trial new fruits one at a time and note any symptoms \u2014 everyone\u2019s triggers differ.   If you keep having symptoms despite adjustments, check in with your provider.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0024-67-03178:8": 2.769717216491699,
            "clueweb22-en0006-69-12013:5": 2.6067752838134766,
            "clueweb22-en0041-22-06879:5": 2.547212839126587,
            "clueweb22-en0021-74-19197:1": 2.4440035820007324,
            "clueweb22-en0024-67-03178:9": 2.3595542907714844
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s reassuring. I like apples, kiwi, and watermelon, so I\u2019ve got good options! Are there any specific drinks I should stay away from if I\u2019m prone to reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some good habits to help someone with stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 4,
          "text": "Good choices \u2014 and yes, there are some drinks to avoid and better swaps.  Drinks to avoid or limit - Carbonated/sparkling water and sodas (they cause burping/gastric distention and can worsen reflux) - Citrus juices (orange, grapefruit, lemon/lime) and very tart fruit juices - Coffee and other caffeinated drinks (tea, some colas) \u2014 they can relax the LES; decaf may be easier on you - Alcohol and chocolate drinks - Sports drinks with citric acid - Flavored waters that are acidic  Better options - Plain water (or alkaline water for some people) - Caffeine\u2011free herbal teas (avoid peppermint/spearmint) \u2014 chamomile, licorice, slippery elm - Low\u2011fat milk or plant milks (almond, coconut) - Smoothies made with non\u2011citrus fruits and yogurt/kefir  Practical tip: avoid drinking large amounts right before bed \u2014 stop fluids 2\u20133 hours before lying down if possible. If symptoms persist, see your provider.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0046-54-03909:9": 1.0660117864608765,
            "clueweb22-en0024-67-03178:0": 0.9529029726982117,
            "clueweb22-en0038-56-11940:9": 0.9261807203292847,
            "clueweb22-en0024-67-03178:9": 0.7093778848648071,
            "clueweb22-en0036-22-02027:11": 0.704261064529419
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. I drink a lot of sparkling water and coffee, so I\u2019ll try cutting back. Is acid reflux mostly caused by food and drink, or are there other reasons?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "It\u2019s a mix \u2014 food and drink are common triggers, but they\u2019re not the only causes. Acid reflux happens when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is weak or when stomach contents are pushed upward. Non\u2011diet causes include: - Anatomical issues (hiatal hernia) - Excess abdominal pressure (obesity, pregnancy) - Certain medications (some blood\u2011pressure, anti\u2011anxiety, pain drugs) - Smoking and alcohol (they relax the LES) - Overeating, eating right before bed, or exercising after a big meal  What helps: lose excess weight, quit smoking, avoid tight clothing, eat smaller meals, stop eating/drinking 2\u20133 hours before lying down, stay upright after meals, prop up the head of the bed, and cut triggers like coffee and fizzy drinks. If lifestyle changes don\u2019t control symptoms, see a provider \u2014 they can evaluate (endoscopy, pH testing) and offer meds or procedures as needed.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0016-58-19315:4": -5.128251075744629,
            "clueweb22-en0010-06-10366:4": -5.695551872253418,
            "clueweb22-en0026-96-04891:0": -5.921568870544434,
            "clueweb22-en0021-88-06442:8": -5.976895809173584,
            "clueweb22-en0032-30-08984:2": -6.047464370727539
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019ve gained weight lately and definitely eat late, so I should probably work on those. Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are there any ODC medications for stomach acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "I assume you mean OTC (over\u2011the\u2011counter) options \u2014 yes, there are several types:  - Antacids (fast relief): calcium carbonate (Tums), magnesium hydroxide (Milk of Magnesia), sodium bicarbonate (baking soda/Alka\u2011Seltzer), bismuth subsalicylate (Pepto\u2011Bismol). Good for occasional, immediate symptoms. Watch sodium content and interactions (baking soda) and use moderately.  - H2 blockers (longer relief, slower onset): famotidine (Pepcid), cimetidine (Tagamet). Help for several hours and useful for intermittent heartburn. May lose effect with chronic use.  - OTC proton\u2011pump inhibitors (PPIs) (for frequent heartburn): omeprazole (Prilosec OTC), lansoprazole (Prevacid), esomeprazole (Nexium OTC). Best for frequent symptoms (\u22652 days/week); usually taken as a 14\u2011day course and can take a few days to work.  Warnings: talk with your provider before starting if you take other meds (drug interactions), have kidney disease, are pregnant, or use them long\u2011term (PPIs have possible risks with prolonged use). See a doctor promptly for alarm symptoms (difficulty swallowing, weight loss, vomiting blood or black stools, severe/chest pain). Lifestyle changes (lose weight, avoid late meals) remain first\u2011line.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0039-19-04384:0": -4.714109420776367,
            "clueweb22-en0019-51-15848:5": -6.674093246459961,
            "clueweb22-en0002-26-16360:1": -6.719527244567871,
            "clueweb22-en0036-30-10562:3": -6.96628999710083,
            "clueweb22-en0026-03-07698:0": -7.034303188323975
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s clear. How do those medications actually work in the body to help with reflux symptoms?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do medication for stomach acid reflux work?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short version: they work at different steps \u2014 antacids neutralize acid already in the stomach, H2 blockers reduce a signaling pathway that tells the stomach to make acid, and PPIs shut off the final acid\u2011producing pump.  More detail: - Antacids (Tums, Maalox) chemically neutralize or adsorb gastric acid, so they act within minutes but wear off quickly.   - H2 blockers (famotidine, cimetidine) block histamine H2 receptors on the stomach\u2019s parietal cells, lowering acid production for several hours (onset ~30\u201360 min). With chronic use their effect can wane.   - Proton\u2011pump inhibitors (omeprazole, lansoprazole) irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase (\u201cproton pump\u201d) on parietal cells \u2014 the final step in acid secretion. They\u2019re the most potent, give ~24\u2011hour acid suppression, but take a day or more to reach full effect.  Practical: antacids = fast relief, H2 = intermittent control, PPIs = best for frequent symptoms. Talk with your provider about long\u2011term use and drug interactions.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0001-93-13662:6": 1.3123722076416016,
            "clueweb22-en0016-54-03535:3": 1.028617262840271,
