    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. I drink a lot of sparkling water and coffee, so I\u2019ll try cutting back. Is acid reflux mostly caused by food and drink, or are there other reasons?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does acid reflux happen? Does acid reflux have a reason?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "It\u2019s a mix \u2014 food and drink are common triggers, but they\u2019re not the only causes. Acid reflux happens when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is weak or when stomach contents are pushed upward. Non\u2011diet causes include: - Anatomical issues (hiatal hernia) - Excess abdominal pressure (obesity, pregnancy) - Certain medications (some blood\u2011pressure, anti\u2011anxiety, pain drugs) - Smoking and alcohol (they relax the LES) - Overeating, eating right before bed, or exercising after a big meal  What helps: lose excess weight, quit smoking, avoid tight clothing, eat smaller meals, stop eating/drinking 2\u20133 hours before lying down, stay upright after meals, prop up the head of the bed, and cut triggers like coffee and fizzy drinks. If lifestyle changes don\u2019t control symptoms, see a provider \u2014 they can evaluate (endoscopy, pH testing) and offer meds or procedures as needed.",
